I was wrong, and your test shows it. `const` doesn't make an object immutable.

`const` only prevents **reassigning the binding**, meaning the variable can't be pointed at a different value:

```js
const o = {a: 1};
o.a = 2;        // OK: mutating the object the binding points to
o = {a: 3};     // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable
```

The object itself stays fully mutable. You can add, change, or delete properties.

If you want immutability, you have a few options:

```js
// Shallow freeze: top-level properties can't be changed
const o = Object.freeze({a: 1, nested: {b: 2}});
o.a = 2;          // silently ignored (throws TypeError in strict mode)
o.nested.b = 3;   // still works, because freeze is shallow

// Deep freeze: recurse yourself
function deepFreeze(obj) {
  Object.values(obj).forEach(v => {
    if (typeof v === "object" && v !== null) deepFreeze(v);
  });
  return Object.freeze(obj);
}
```

Other approaches:
- `structuredClone` plus copy-on-write patterns, where you never mutate and always create new objects.
- Libraries like Immer or Immutable.js.
- In TypeScript, `as const` or `Readonly<T>` / `readonly` give compile-time protection only, with no runtime enforcement.

